1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,887
Can an AI assistant become an expert simply by learning a new skill?

2
00:00:05,087 --> 00:00:13,236
Claude Skills make AI workflows more structured, but there is an important difference between following instructions and understanding a problem.

3
00:00:13,436 --> 00:00:16,103
This is Liam, narrating for Deepa.

4
00:00:17,425 --> 00:00:23,590
A Claude Skill is a reusable package of instructions and supporting resources for a particular task.

5
00:00:23,790 --> 00:00:29,401
Its SKILL.md file explains when to use the skill and how to approach the workflow.

6
00:00:29,601 --> 00:00:33,228
Some packages include scripts or reference material.

7
00:00:33,428 --> 00:00:38,697
These instructions guide the assistant; they do not automatically give it expert judgment.

8
00:00:40,008 --> 00:00:44,680
Web Artifacts Builder guides Claude through building interactive web artifacts.

9
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:50,363
It provides a structured path from setting up a project to developing and bundling the interface.

10
00:00:50,563 --> 00:00:53,102
That can make the work more consistent.

11
00:00:53,302 --> 00:01:00,918
But an interface that appears on screen still needs testing for functionality, accessibility, code quality, and a usable experience.

12
00:01:02,258 --> 00:01:06,653
Webapp Testing supports checking local web applications with Playwright.

13
00:01:06,853 --> 00:01:14,341
The workflow can inspect a page, exercise user interactions, and capture useful evidence such as screenshots or browser logs.

14
00:01:14,541 --> 00:01:18,594
Passing tests is valuable evidence about the cases tested.

15
00:01:18,794 --> 00:01:24,405
It is not proof that every device, unexpected input, or user journey is free of defects.

16
00:01:25,717 --> 00:01:31,178
The XLSX skill supports spreadsheet work with tools such as pandas and openpyxl.

17
00:01:31,378 --> 00:01:36,029
It helps organize data, write formulas, and validate workbook outputs.

18
00:01:36,229 --> 00:01:42,415
Yet a formula can calculate successfully while using the wrong input or an unrealistic assumption.

19
00:01:42,615 --> 00:01:46,669
Technical correctness and business accuracy are different questions.

20
00:01:46,869 --> 00:01:50,026
Someone still needs to check what the numbers mean.

21
00:01:51,342 --> 00:01:56,782
Slack GIF Creator supplies guidance and utilities for creating animated GIFs for Slack.

22
00:01:56,982 --> 00:02:01,355
It supports frame assembly, animation concepts, and technical checks.

23
00:02:01,555 --> 00:02:05,054
A file can meet those checks without communicating well.

24
00:02:05,254 --> 00:02:11,526
Timing, readability, context, and the meaning a viewer takes away still need a thoughtful visual review.

25
00:02:12,842 --> 00:02:18,559
Across these examples, skills can improve consistency, repeatability, and task execution.

26
00:02:18,759 --> 00:02:24,775
They do not automatically provide domain expertise, independent judgment, or guaranteed accuracy.

27
00:02:24,975 --> 00:02:30,863
Ask two questions: did the workflow run as intended, and is the result suitable for the real problem?

28
00:02:31,063 --> 00:02:34,242
Those are related, but they are not the same test.

29
00:02:35,550 --> 00:02:42,441
Try this with a task you know: name one thing an automated check can verify, and one thing that still needs your judgment.

30
00:02:42,641 --> 00:02:51,942
AI Skills can make automation more reliable, but the quality of the outcome still depends on clear instructions, meaningful validation, and human judgment.

31
00:02:52,142 --> 00:02:54,339
Liam, narrating for Deepa.

32
00:02:54,539 --> 00:02:56,694
Humanitarians AI.
